# AI Prompting Guide: Build the Team Collaboration Platform in 3 Weeks

This guide shows you how to instruct an AI coding tool (Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot, Windsurf, or a chat assistant) so it builds your app one working piece at a time, in line with the 3-week plan.

**Stack:** Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript, PostgreSQL (Neon), Prisma, Auth.js, Pusher, Cloudflare R2, Resend, Tailwind + shadcn/ui, hosted on Vercel.

---

## 1. The core rules

1. **One vertical slice per prompt.** A slice is: schema change, server logic, UI, and a test for one feature. Never ask for "the whole app".
2. **Give context every time.** The AI forgets between sessions. A `CONTEXT.md` file in your repo fixes this (section 2).
3. **Be specific about who can do what.** Permissions are the most common source of AI mistakes.
4. **Ask for a plan first, code second.** Plans are cheap to correct, code is not.
5. **Run it before moving on.** Each slice must work in the browser and pass `npm run build` before you start the next one.
6. **Commit after every working slice.** If the next prompt breaks things, you can roll back in seconds.
7. **Review anything that touches auth, permissions, file uploads, or secrets yourself.** Read it line by line.

---

## 2. Step 0: Create your CONTEXT.md (do this on Day 1)

Create this file in the project root and paste it (or point your tool to it) at the start of every session. In Claude Code you can name it `CLAUDE.md`; in Cursor, put it in `.cursor/rules`.

```markdown
# Project: Team Collaboration Platform (Basecamp-style)

## Purpose
Internal company tool: admin manages users and roles; employees chat in real time,
get assigned tasks, and share files inside projects.

## Stack
- Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript (strict)
- PostgreSQL on Neon, Prisma ORM (pooled DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL for migrations)
- Auth.js with Prisma adapter, credentials login, session includes user id and role
- Pusher Channels for real-time chat
- Cloudflare R2 (S3 API) with presigned URLs for files
- Resend for email
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- Zod for validation
- Hosted on Vercel (serverless: NO WebSockets, NO local disk storage)

## Roles
ADMIN, MANAGER, EMPLOYEE. All permission checks go through `can(user, action, resource)`
in `src/lib/permissions.ts`. Never check roles inline in components.

## Conventions
- Mutations use server actions in `src/actions/<feature>.ts`
- Every server action: 1) get session 2) validate input with Zod 3) check `can()` 4) do the work
- Prisma client is a singleton in `src/lib/db.ts`
- Components in `src/components/<feature>/`, pages in `src/app/`
- No `any`. No secrets in client code. All env vars documented in `.env.example`
- Use shadcn/ui components before writing custom ones
- Loading, empty, and error states are required for every list and form

## Definition of done for any feature
- Works in the browser
- `npm run lint` and `npm run build` pass
- Permissions enforced on the server
- At least one test for the main path and one for a forbidden case
```

Update this file whenever you make a decision. It is your project's memory.

---

## 3. The prompt template

Use this structure for every feature prompt. Fill in the brackets.

```text
Read CONTEXT.md first.

TASK
Implement [feature name].

BACKGROUND
[1-3 sentences: what already exists and what this builds on.]

REQUIREMENTS
- [What the user can do, in plain language]
- [Another behaviour]
- [Edge cases, e.g. empty state, deactivated users, large input]

PERMISSIONS
- ADMIN: [what they can do]
- MANAGER: [what they can do]
- EMPLOYEE: [what they can do]
- Anyone not a member of the project: no access

DATA
[Prisma models or fields involved. Say if the schema needs to change.]

UI
[Pages and components needed. Reference existing components to reuse.]

CONSTRAINTS
- Follow the conventions in CONTEXT.md
- Do not change unrelated files
- Do not install new packages without telling me why

PROCESS
1. First, give me a short plan listing files you will create or change. Wait for my OK.
2. Then implement it.
3. Then tell me exactly how to test it manually.
```

The "Wait for my OK" step is what keeps you in control.

---

## 4. Day-by-day prompts

These match the 3-week plan. Copy each one, adjust it to your needs, and always use the template above around it.

### Week 1: Foundation, users, roles, projects

**Day 1: Setup and schema**

```text
Set up a new Next.js App Router project with TypeScript strict mode, Tailwind, and
shadcn/ui. Add Prisma configured for PostgreSQL with DATABASE_URL (pooled) and
DIRECT_URL (migrations). Create src/lib/db.ts with a singleton Prisma client.

Then write the full Prisma schema for: User (role enum ADMIN/MANAGER/EMPLOYEE,
isActive), Project, ProjectMember, Channel (type PROJECT/DM), ChannelMember
(lastReadAt), Message (optional parentId), Task (status enum TODO/IN_PROGRESS/REVIEW/
DONE, priority, assigneeId, createdById, dueDate), TaskComment, File, Attachment
(links a file to a message or task), Notification, ActivityLog.
Add sensible indexes and onDelete rules. Add the Auth.js models.

Give me a plan first. Also create .env.example and a seed script that creates one
admin user, 3 employees, 1 project, and sample data.
```

**Day 2: Authentication and permissions**

```text
Implement Auth.js with credentials login (email + password hashed with argon2 or
bcrypt) using the Prisma adapter. The session must include user id and role.
Deactivated users (isActive=false) must not be able to log in.

Add middleware that protects everything except /login. Create src/lib/permissions.ts
exporting can(user, action, resource) with a typed list of actions, and implement the
role table from CONTEXT.md. Write unit tests for can() covering every role.
```

**Day 3: Admin user management**

```text
Build the admin area at /admin/users. Admin can: list users (search + filter by role),
create a user (name, email, role), edit name and role, and deactivate or reactivate.
Creating a user sends an invitation email via Resend with a one-time set-password
link that expires in 48 hours. Also implement forgot-password using the same token
mechanism. Only ADMIN can access these pages and actions; enforce it in the server
actions as well as in the UI. Prevent an admin from deactivating or demoting
themselves.
```

**Day 4: App shell and profile**

```text
Build the authenticated app shell: sidebar (Projects, Chat, My Tasks, Files, Admin
if admin), top bar with notification bell placeholder and user menu, responsive on
mobile with a collapsible sidebar. Add a profile page where users edit their name
and upload an avatar (use presigned upload to R2, 2 MB limit, images only).
```

**Day 5: Projects**

```text
Implement projects. ADMIN and MANAGER can create projects. A project has name,
description, and members. Project owners and admins can add or remove members and
archive the project. Members see the project on their dashboard; non-members get a
404. Creating a project also creates its default chat channel and adds the creator
as a member. Build the project overview page with tabs: Chat, Tasks, Files, People.
```

### Week 2: Chat and tasks

**Day 7: Chat backend**

```text
Implement real-time chat with Pusher Channels. Use PRIVATE channels named
private-channel-{id} and a /api/pusher/auth route that verifies the user is a
ChannelMember. The sendMessage server action must: verify membership, validate the
body (1-4000 chars), save to Postgres, then trigger a Pusher event. Add a
cursor-paginated getMessages(channelId, cursor) function that returns the newest 30
first. Explain how I should set up the Pusher account and env vars.
```

**Day 8: Chat UI**

```text
Build the chat UI for a channel: message list with sender avatar, name and time,
infinite scroll upward to load older messages, auto-scroll to bottom for new messages
(unless the user scrolled up), a composer (Enter sends, Shift+Enter newline), optimistic
sending with a failed state and retry, and edit/delete for the user's own messages.
Subscribe to the Pusher channel and de-duplicate messages by id.
```

**Day 9: Direct messages and unread counts**

```text
Add direct messages. Starting a DM with a user finds or creates a DM channel with
exactly those two members. Add unread counts per channel using ChannelMember.lastReadAt
(update it when the user opens or scrolls a channel). Show unread badges in the
sidebar. Optional if time allows: typing indicator and online presence using Pusher
presence channels.
```

**Day 10: Tasks**

```text
Implement tasks inside projects. Fields: title, description, status, priority,
assignee (must be a project member), due date. ADMIN and MANAGER can create and
assign. An EMPLOYEE can change the status of tasks assigned to them but not reassign.
Build a list view with filters (status, assignee, due date) and a simple Kanban board
with drag and drop between status columns. Moving a card must call a server action
that checks permissions.
```

**Day 11: Task detail and My Tasks**

```text
Build the task detail page (or side panel) with editable fields, a comment thread,
attachments placeholder, and an activity history. Add a "My Tasks" page grouped by
Overdue, Today, This week, Later, with quick status change.
```

**Day 12: Notifications**

```text
Implement notifications: create a Notification row when a task is assigned to a user,
when a task assigned to them changes status, and when someone @mentions them in chat.
Show a bell with an unread count and a dropdown list with mark-as-read. Send an email
via Resend for task assignment only. Deliver new in-app notifications live through a
per-user private Pusher channel.
```

### Week 3: Files, polish, launch

**Day 13: File storage**

```text
Implement file storage with Cloudflare R2 using presigned URLs. Flow: client calls
requestUpload(projectId, name, size, mimeType) -> server checks membership, validates
size (max 25 MB) and an allowlist of types, creates a pending File row, returns a
presigned PUT URL -> client uploads directly to R2 -> client calls confirmUpload(id).
Downloads use short-lived presigned GET URLs after a permission check. The bucket is
private. Build the project Files tab with upload (drag and drop, progress bar),
list, download, and delete (uploader, manager, or admin only).
```

**Day 14: Attachments and previews**

```text
Allow attaching uploaded files to chat messages and tasks using the Attachment model.
Show image thumbnails and an inline preview for images and PDFs. Optional: simple
folders inside a project's Files tab.
```

**Day 15: Admin dashboard and search**

```text
Build the admin dashboard: total users by role, active projects, tasks by status,
overdue tasks, storage used, and recent activity from ActivityLog. Add basic global
search (people, projects, tasks, file names) that only returns items the current user
is allowed to see.
```

**Day 16: Security review**

```text
Act as a security reviewer. Audit this codebase for: missing permission checks in
server actions and route handlers, IDOR issues (accessing records by guessing ids),
unvalidated input, secrets exposed to the client, missing rate limiting on login and
message sending, and unsafe file handling. List each issue with file and line, severity,
and a fix. Do not change code yet.
```

Then fix issues one at a time with separate prompts.

**Day 17: Testing and polish**

```text
Write Playwright end-to-end tests for: admin creates a user, employee logs in,
employee sends a chat message that a second browser sees live, manager assigns a
task, employee updates its status, a file is uploaded and downloaded, and an
employee is blocked from the admin page. Then review loading, empty and error
states across the app and list anything missing.
```

**Day 18: Deploy**

```text
Prepare for production on Vercel. Give me a checklist covering: environment variables,
Neon pooled vs direct connection strings, running prisma migrate deploy, prisma
generate in the build, R2 CORS configuration, Pusher keys, Resend domain verification,
Sentry setup, and a first-admin seed script. Also write a README and a one-page user
guide for admins and employees.
```

